st.mjs\\n\\tcapability-lifecycle-unpinned-codex-4029.test.mjs\\n\\tcheck-release-needed-2175.test.mjs\\n\\tcheck-web-archive.test.mjs\\n\\t[860 truncated]\\n.dockerignore\\n.env.example\\n.gitattributes\\n.gitignore\\n.gitkeep\\n.gitpod.Dockerfile\\n.gitpod.yml\\n.jscpd.json\\n.lenv.example\\n.lycheeignore\\n.npmrc\\n.prettierignore\\n.prettierrc\\n.secretlintignore\\n.secretlintrc.json\\nanalyze-issue.md\\nCHANGELOG.md\\nclaude-runtime.mjs\\ncleanup-test-repos.mjs\\ncreate-test-repo.mjs\\ncspell.json\\ndo.mjs\\ndocker-compose.yml\\ndocker-restore-auth.sh\\ndocker-solve.sh\\nDockerfile\\nDockerfile.dind\\nDockerfile.e2e\\nDockerfile.formal-ai\\neslint.config.mjs\\ngitpod-backup-auth.sh\\ngitpod-restore-auth.sh\\nLICENSE\\npackage-lock.json\\npackage.json\\nREADME.hi.md\\nREADME.md\\nREADME.ru.md\\nREADME.zh.md\\n</files>\"},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"You are an AI issue solver using @link-assistant/agent. Prefer minimal local CPU, memory, and disk use during development.\\nGeneral guidelines.\\n   - When you execute commands and the output becomes large, save the logs to files for easier review.\\n   - When running commands, avoid setting a timeout yourself. Let them run as long as needed.\\n   - When running sudo commands, especially package installations, run them in the background to avoid timeout issues.\\n   - When CI is failing, download the logs locally and investigate them carefully.\\n   - When a code or log file has more than 1500 lines, read it in chunks of 1500 lines.\\n   - When diagnosing a complex problem, prefer targeted tracing and enable verbose output only where useful.\\n   - When you create debug, test, or example scripts while fixing an issue, keep them in ./examples and/or ./experiments so you can reuse them later.\\n   - When you test assumptions, keep experiment scripts in ./experiments.\\n   - When an experiment demonstrates a real-world use case of the software, add it to ./examples.\\nInitial research.\\n   - When you start, outline the work and track requirements and verification in your plan.\\n   - When you read issue, read all details and comments thoroughly.\\n   - When you see screenshots or images in issue descriptions, pull request descriptions, comments, or discussions, download the image to a local file first, then use the Read tool to view and analyze it. Before reading downloaded images with the Read tool, verify that the file is a valid image rather than HTML by using a CLI tool such as the 'file' command. When the file command shows \\\"HTML\\\", \\\"text\\\", or \\\"ASCII text\\\", the download failed, so do not call Read on that file. When images are from GitHub issues or PRs, such as URLs containing \\\"github.com/user-attachments\\\", use: curl -L -H \\\"Authorization: token $(gh auth token)\\\" -o <filename> \\\"<url>\\\"\\n   - When you need issue details, use gh issue view https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4135.\\n   - When you need related code, use gh search code --owner link-assistant [keywords].\\n   - When you need repo context, read files in your working directory.\\n   - When you study related work, study the most recent related pull requests.\\n   - When the issue is not defined clearly enough, write a comment with clarifying questions.\\n   - When accessing GitHub Gists, use gh gist view command instead of direct URL fetching.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug, find the actual root cause first and run as many experiments as needed.\\n   - When you are fixing a bug and the code does not have enough tracing or logs, add them and keep them in the code with the default state switched off.\\n   - When you need comments on a pull request, note that GitHub has three different comment types with different API endpoints:\\n      1. PR review comments (inline code comments): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4137/comments --paginate\\n      2. PR conversation comments (general discussion): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4137/comments --paginate\\n      3. PR reviews (approve/request changes): gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4137/reviews --paginate\\n      Note: The command \\\"gh pr view --json comments\\\" only returns conversation comments and misses review comments.\\n   - When you need the latest comments on the issue, use gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4135/comments --paginate.\\nSolution development and testing.\\n   - When coding, commit each atomic step that is useful on its own to the pull request branch so interrupted work remains preserved in the pull request.\\n   - When implementing changes, prefer drafting code for all requirements before starting verification, then use focused checks and existing CI/CD coverage.\\n   - When validating changes, prefer a single relevant test or small focused checks; check whether the command triggers a full source build before running it.\\n   - When CI/CD already covers full test suites or CPU-intensive builds, prefer leaving those to CI/CD and checking results for the current commit; avoid duplicating them locally unless user requirements or uncovered failures call for it.\\n   - When CI/CD coverage is missing and adding it was not requested, suggest https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/main/docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md; let the repository owner decide whether to add it, and meanwhile complete required validation with available resources.\\n   - When writing tests, prefer small cases with mocks and the existing integration framework.\\n   - When you test solution draft, include automated checks in pr.\\n   - When you write or modify tests, consider setting reasonable timeouts at test, suite, and CI job levels so failures surface quickly instead of hanging.\\n   - When you see repeated test timeout patterns in CI, investigate the root cause rather than increasing timeouts.\\n   - When the issue is unclear, write a comment on the issue with questions.\\n   - When you encounter any problems that you are unable to solve yourself, write a comment to the pull request asking for help.\\n   - When you need human help, use gh pr comment 4137 --body \\\"your message\\\" to comment on existing PR.\\nReproducible testing.\\n   - When fixing a bug, prefer a minimal regression test that fails before the fix and passes afterward.\\n   - When encountering UI bugs, capture a screenshot showing the problem state, then create a visual regression test or manual verification screenshot after the fix.\\n   - When submitting a fix, include in the PR description: (1) how to reproduce the issue, (2) the automated test that verifies the fix, (3) before/after screenshots for UI issues.\\n   - When a bug fix does not have a reproducing test, treat the fix as incomplete because regressions can occur later without notice.\\nPreparing pull request.\\n   - When you code, follow contributing guidelines.\\n   - When you commit, write clear message.\\n   - When you need examples of style, use gh pr list --repo link-assistant/hive-mind --state merged --search [keywords].\\n   - When you open pr, describe solution draft and include tests.\\n   - When there is a package with version and GitHub Actions workflows for automatic release, update the version in your pull request to prepare for next release.\\n   - When you update existing pr 4137, use gh pr edit to modify title and description.\\n   - When you finalize the pull request:\\n      check that the pull request title and description are updated (the PR may start with a [WIP] prefix and a placeholder description that should be replaced with the actual title and description of the changes),\\n      follow style from merged prs for code, title, and description,\\n      check that no uncommitted changes corresponding to the original requirements are left behind,\\n      check that the default branch is merged into the pull request branch,\\n      check that all CI checks are passing if they exist before you finish, including checks that already fail on the default branch (when a check needs a human action, make CI on the pull request pass without it and CI on the default branch fail with an explicit message saying what to do),\\n      check for latest comments on the issue and pull request to ensure no recent feedback was missed,\\n      double-check that all changes in the pull request address the original requirements of the issue,\\n      check for newly introduced bugs in the pull request by carefully reading gh pr diff,\\n      check that no previously existing features were removed without an explicit request in the issue description, issue comments, or pull request comments.\\n   - When you finish implementation, use gh pr ready 4137.\\nWorkflow and collaboration.\\n   - When you check branch, verify with git branch --show-current.\\n   - When you push, push only to branch issue-4135-24ce02ee8e56.\\n   - When you finish, create a pull request from branch issue-4135-24ce02ee8e56.\\n   - When pr 4137 already exists for this branch, update it instead of creating new one.\\n   - When you organize workflow, use pull requests instead of direct merges to default branch (main or master).\\n   - When you manage commits, preserve commit history for later analysis.\\n   - When you contribute, keep repository history forward-moving with regular commits, pushes, and reverts if needed.\\n   - When you face conflict that you cannot resolve yourself, ask for help.\\n   - When you collaborate, respect branch protections by working only on issue-4135-24ce02ee8e56.\\n   - When you mention a result, include the pull request URL or comment URL.\\nSelf review.\\n   - When you check your solution draft, verify git status shows a clean working tree with no uncommitted changes.\\n   - When you compare with repo style, use gh pr diff [number].\\n   - When you finalize, confirm code, tests, and description are consistent.\\nGitHub CLI command patterns.\\n   - When fetching lists from GitHub API, use the --paginate flag to ensure all results are returned (GitHub returns max 30 per page by default).\\n   - When adding PR comment, use gh pr comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When adding issue comment, use gh issue comment NUMBER --body \\\"text\\\" --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When viewing PR details, use gh pr view NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO.\\n   - When filtering with jq, use gh api repos/link-assistant/hive-mind/pulls/4137/comments --paginate --jq 'reverse | .[0:5]'.\\nPlaywright MCP usage (browser automation via MCP tools).\\n   - When you develop frontend web applications (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, React, Vue, Angular, etc.), use Playwright MCP tools to test the UI in a real browser.\\n   - When WebFetch tool fails to retrieve expected content (e.g., returns empty content, JavaScript-rendered pages, or login-protected pages), use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for web browsing.\\n   - When WebSearch tool fails or returns insufficient results, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot) as a fallback for internet search.\\n   - When you need to interact with dynamic web pages that require JavaScript execution, use Playwright MCP tools.\\n   - When you need to visually verify how a web page looks or take screenshots, use browser_take_screenshot from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you need to fill forms, click buttons, or perform user interactions on web pages, use Playwright MCP tools (browser_click, browser_type, browser_fill_form).\\n   - When you need to test responsive design or different viewport sizes, use browser_resize from Playwright MCP.\\n   - When you finish using the browser, close it with browser_close to free resources.\\n   - When reproducing UI bugs, use browser_take_screenshot to capture the problem state before implementing any fix.\\n   - When fixing UI bugs, take before/after screenshots to provide visual evidence of the fix for human verification.\\n   - When creating UI tests, save baseline screenshots to the repository for visual regression testing.\\n   - When verifying UI fixes, compare screenshots to ensure the fix does not introduce unintended visual changes.\\nIssue completion goal.\\n   - Do not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\n   - Try to set this exact objective with a native goal-setting API or tool before working, and keep it active until every acceptance criterion is verified. Codex exposes thread/goal/set through its app-server API when available; do not invent an exec --goal flag. If the running tool provides no native goal API, keep the objective and checklist in your work plan across turns and compaction.\\n   - Read the complete issue, every comment, every required sub-issue, and all pull request conversation comments, inline comments, and reviews. Inventory each requirement, including prose requirements and later feedback, and map each one to implementation and verification evidence.\\n   - Passing CI, a partial implementation, an upstream report, or a workaround does not complete an unmet acceptance criterion. Continue authorized work. If an external blocker cannot be resolved, report the exact blocked criteria and evidence, leave them unfinished, and keep the pull request unmerged.\\n   - Preserve a positive closing keyword in the pull request description for the original issue and every required issue. Repeat the keyword per issue, use the correct repository for foreign issues, and keep references outside examples or code blocks. A negated sentence (for example \\\"does not close\\\" before an issue reference) is not a closing reference. Mention issues you reported to other repositories without a keyword: GitHub also closes an issue linked right after \\\"fix:\\\", as in \\\"a suggested fix: <issue URL>\\\".\\n   - When you mark the pull request ready or push to it, wait up to 15 minutes for review apps such as chatgpt-codex-connector[bot], coderabbitai[bot] (👀 means reviewing; 👍 or \\\"has_unresolved_findings\\\":false means no findings), then address every finding: fix it and resolve the thread, or reply with evidence.\\nLocation privacy: Keep the user's and host's geolocation private. Avoid collecting, inferring, or transmitting their location, including IP-geolocation lookups, GPS/browser location permissions, local timezone or region probes, and user_location hints in search or model requests. Use location-neutral searches. Propagate this policy to delegated agents and browser/MCP tools. Locations explicitly present in task content may be used to discuss that content; they do not authorize discovery or disclosure of the user's or host's location.\\nIssue to solve: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4135\\nYour prepared branch: issue-4135-24ce02ee8e56\\nYour prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1795421014635\\nYour prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/pull/4137\\nCollect data related about the issue to this repository, make sure we compile that data into the ./dev/log/issues/4135/pulls/4137 folder.\\nDo not stop until every requirement of the issue and all required sub-issues is fully implemented and verified in this single pull request, in the widest requested scope, with no requested work deferred; work that is truly blocked must be reported as blocked and left unfinished, never presented as done.\\nProceed.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"I’ll run `gh`.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_1f1d95fd550e81ca\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"command\\\":\\\"gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4135' --json title --jq .title && echo && gh issue view 'https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/4135' --json body --jq .body; printf '\\\\n__formal_ai_exit=%s\\\\n' $?\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"I’ll run `gh`.\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_1f1d95fd550e81ca\",\"content\":\"Honor the pinned manager package version for SSH maintenance\\n\\nA 2.48.0 operator CLI sends its own Bash program via `--host`, but packaged maintenance follows the SSH host's `hive-manager`/ `hive` installation (without checking its package version) or falls back to npm on that host. The invoking shell's `HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION` is not carried in the remote command. This lets the wrapper and storage/reporting implementation have different versions. See [remote dispatch and package fallback](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/blob/v2.48.0/src/hive-manager/cli.sh#L117-L189).\\n\\nTo reproduce on a disposable OpenSSH target with Node and npm and no Hive Mind package on its noninteractive PATH, invoke `HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION=2.48.0 /path/to/pinned/hive-manager --host STAGE deploy disk --help`. Put a test npm shim after Node's bin directory that records argument boundaries and exits without network access. The remote exec requests `@link-assistant/hive-mind@latest`. With a different installed package that has the storage module on the target PATH, dispatch prefers that package even with an explicit local fallback pin. Local unstreamed invocation from the pinned package uses its own installed module.\\n\\nPlease carry an explicit package pin across SSH, verify an installed package against it before dispatch, and expose the effective package/version in diagnostics. Keep the shipped CLI/image and container-side version semantics explicit in the runbook. The migration currently installs the exact target package and checks its PATH acquisition separately.\\n\\nMigration context is link-assistant/hive-mind-control-center#1 and link-assistant/hive-mind-control-center#2.\\n\\n\\n__formal_ai_exit=0\\n\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me search the code for HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_0ee3288d48e86715\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"grep\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"pattern\\\":\\\"HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_0ee3288d48e86715\",\"content\":\"Found 2 matches\\n/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1795421014635/src/hive-manager/cli.sh:\\n  Line 188:   local version=${HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION:-latest}\\n\\n/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1795421014635/docs/deployment/hive-manager.md:\\n  Line 13: For interactive commands, redirects, or repeated use, save the file and invoke it with Bash. Replace `main` with a release reference or commit SHA to pin the download. Bash and standard Linux utilities are enough for deployment preparation and local backup. Docker operations also require a local Docker Engine on the deployment host and permission to access it. Install Node.js and Hive Mind to run package maintenance commands on the host, or use `deploy` to run them inside the image. When a standalone maintenance command is missing locally and npm is available, the manager uses `npm exec` with the published package. Set `HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION` to pin that fallback.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1795421014635/docs/deployment/hive-manager.md and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_d43f4195ad2ff36d\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1795421014635/docs/deployment/hive-manager.md\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_d43f4195ad2ff36d\",\"content\":\"<file>\\n00001| # Hive Mind Manager\\n00002| \\n00003| `hive-manager` runs on a local deployment host, over SSH from a developer machine, and inside Hive Mind images. It ships with `@link-assistant/hive-mind`. To use a single copy without installing the package, download the script or stream it into Bash.\\n00004| \\n00005| ```bash\\n00006| npm install -g @link-assistant/hive-mind\\n00007| hive-manager --help\\n00008| \\n00009| curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/main/src/hive-manager/cli.sh | bash\\n00010| curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/main/src/hive-manager/cli.sh | bash -s -- deploy --help\\n00011| ```\\n00012| \\n00013| For interactive commands, redirects, or repeated use, save the file and invoke it with Bash. Replace `main` with a release reference or commit SHA to pin the download. Bash and standard Linux utilities are enough for deployment preparation and local backup. Docker operations also require a local Docker Engine on the deployment host and permission to access it. Install Node.js and Hive Mind to run package maintenance commands on the host, or use `deploy` to run them inside the image. When a standalone maintenance command is missing locally and npm is available, the manager uses `npm exec` with the published package. Set `HIVE_MANAGER_PACKAGE_VERSION` to pin that fallback.\\n00014| \\n00015| ## JavaScript library\\n00016| \\n00017| Install `@link-assistant/hive-mind` in your project and import the public `@link-assistant/hive-mind/hive-manager` entry point from Node.js 24 or later. The package includes TypeScript declarations. Importing the entry point runs no commands, writes no files, and produces no output.\\n00018| \\n00019| ```js\\n00020| import { HiveManagerError, previewTaskRecordRepair } from '@link-assistant/hive-mind/hive-manager';\\n00021| \\n00022| try {\\n00023|   const preview = await previewTaskRecordRepair({\\n00024|     uuids: ['40950000-1111-4000-8000-000000000001'],\\n00025|   });\\n00026|   console.log(preview.outcomes);\\n00027| } catch (error) {\\n00028|   if (!(error instanceof HiveManagerError)) throw error;\\n00029|   console.error(error.code, error.message);\\n00030| }\\n00031| ```\\n00032| \\n00033| | Function                                        | Data and result                                                                                                                                                                                       | CLI equivalent                   |\\n00034| | ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\\n00035| | `inspectTasks(tasks, { limit })`                | Counts tasks by state and summarizes recent failures from an array of start-command execution records.                                                                                                | `tasks report [--limit NUMBER]`  |\\n00036| | `groupTaskFailures(tasks, { limit })`           | Groups failed attempts by backend, failure kind and exit code. Both report functions default to 25 results and accept limits from 1 to 1000.                                                          | `tasks report --group`           |\\n00037| | `inspectTaskWindow(tasks, { uuid, attempt })`   | Returns `startedAt` and `finishedAt` in UTC. A running attempt has a `null` finish time.                                                                                                              | `tasks report --window UUID`     |\\n00038| | `collectTaskEvidence(tasks, { uuid, attempt })` | Returns a bounded attempt log summary. Omit `attempt` in either selection function to use the latest attempt.                                                                                         | `tasks report --collect UUID`    |\\n00039| | `previewTaskRecordRepair({ uuids, appFolder })` | Reads the start-command store and inspects running Docker snapshots. Returns `eligible` with a container name or `ambiguous` per matching UUID. No eligible snapshot yields no outcome for that UUID. | `tasks repair-records [UUID...]` |\\n00040| \\n00041| Pass the `executions` array from start-command's JSON task list or an existing snapshot to the inspection functions. `collectTaskEvidence` reads the `logPath` provided by the selected record. Give it trusted records from your task host. Record repair preview requires the start-command 0.44.0 executable (`$`) and Docker on `PATH`. `appFolder` defaults to `START_APP_FOLDER` or `~/.start-command`. An empty or omitted `uuids` array considers all records.\\n00042| \\n00043| Use the `HiveManagerError.code` property to handle invalid selections (`INVALID_INPUT`, `TASK_NOT_FOUND`, `ATTEMPT_NOT_FOUND`, `TASK_WINDOW_UNAVAILABLE`) and repair dependencies or read failures (`START_COMMAND_UNAVAILABLE`, `TASK_RECORDS_UNAVAILABLE`, `DOCKER_INSPECTION_FAILED`). An ambiguous match is a preview outcome. Only `tasks repair-records --apply` adopts a snapshot and attaches a watcher, after rechecking eligibility. Operators continue to own deployment profiles, schedules, incident routes and secrets. Deployment apply, adoption and publication approval use their existing CLI flags.\\n00044| \\n00045| ## First deployment\\n00046| \\n00047| Prepare the persistent state and start the setup container. Run each sign-in separately so it can use your terminal.\\n00048| \\n00049| ```bash\\n00050| hive-manager deploy --setup apply\\n00051| hive-manager deploy auth github\\n00052| hive-manager deploy auth claude\\n00053| hive-manager deploy auth codex\\n00054| ```\\n00055| \\n00056| For a setup-only invocation on a Docker host, run `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/main/src/hive-manager/cli.sh | bash -s -- deploy --setup apply`. Install with npm or Bun, or save the Bash script, before signing in so the command can read your response from stdin.\\n00057| \\n00058| For a Telegram instance, create a private environment file with `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` and the appropriate allowlist/settings, or use an existing private LINO configuration with `--bot-config`. The Docker environment file accepts literal `KEY=value` lines. Use the format documented in [Telegram Bot Configuration](../CONFIGURATION.md) for the LINO file. The bind-mounted LINO file needs to be readable by the image's `box` user while remaining private on the host.\\n00059| \\n00060| ```bash\\n00061| hive-manager deploy --env-file ./telegram.env --dry-run apply\\n00062| hive-manager deploy --env-file ./telegram.env apply\\n00063| hive-manager deploy logs --follow --tail 60\\n00064| ```\\n00065| \\n00066| Choose `--variant dind` for an instance with its own Docker daemon. It uses a privileged container and a dedicated `<name>-dind-data` Docker volume for nested Docker state. Plain deployments default to Screen isolation. To use the host Docker daemon, select `--docker-socket /var/run/docker.sock` instead. A host socket gives the instance control of the host daemon. The DinD variant can also use that option for host image passthrough while keeping its own nested daemon. See [Docker deployment options](../DOCKER.md) for isolation and storage details.\\n00067| \\n00068| Use `--image IMAGE` to select a tag, digest or compatible custom image. `--no-pull` uses an already present image. To run a service other than the Telegram bot, pass its startup command as `apply -- COMMAND ARG...`. Prepare an image without interrupting a running deployment with `deploy prepare`.\\n00069| \\n00070| ## Profiles and remote use\\n00071| \\n00072| Create one profile per deployment on the target. Values are literal. Relative paths are resolved against the profile's directory. Put CLI overrides after `--profile`. The default container name is `hive-mind` and its state directory is `~/.hive-mind` on the target. Set separate names and state paths for multiple instances.\\n00073| \\n00074| ```ini\\n00075| name=hive-production\\n00076| variant=dind\\n00077| state-dir=./state\\n00078| env-file=./telegram.env\\n00079| ```\\n00080| \\n00081| Use ordinary OpenSSH configuration for keys, host verification, DNS, and routing. The manager sends its own Bash script and flags to the host. There is no manager installation required on a deployment host reached through SSH. The profile, state files, credentials and Docker service are all on the target. SSH options go before the command.\\n00082| \\n00083| ```bash\\n00084| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --profile ./hive/production.conf update\\n00085| hive-manager --host deploy@hive.internal --jump gateway,edge-bastion deploy status\\n00086| hive-manager --host hive-production --tty deploy --tty exec -- bash\\n00087| ```\\n00088| \\n00089| Set `ProxyJump` on the destination alias, configure `ProxyCommand` for an SSH transport proxy, or use an already connected VPN. Use `--ssh-config`, `--ssh-port`, and repeated `--ssh-option` for OpenSSH options supplied on the command line. Pass `--tty` on both sides of `deploy` for interactive remote Docker exec. Omit both for pipes and file transfers. See [OpenSSH client configuration](https://man.openbsd.org/ssh_config).\\n00090| \\n00091| ## Headless sign-in\\n00092| \\n00093| Use `auth` for the current account on the selected host and `deploy auth` for the container's `box` account. Sign-in uses the selected environment's Claude, Codex or GitHub CLI. The GitHub flow also requires `gh-setup-git-identity`. Hive Mind images include these tools. For a managed deployment, select the same profile or container name you use for updates so credentials persist in its state mounts. The sign-in commands work over SSH with ordinary stdin and stdout, including through jump hosts.\\n00094| \\n00095| ```bash\\n00096| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --profile ./hive/production.conf auth claude\\n00097| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --profile ./hive/production.conf auth codex\\n00098| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --profile ./hive/production.conf auth github\\n00099| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --profile ./hive/production.conf auth claude status\\n00100| ```\\n00101| \\n00102| - **Claude**. Open the authorization URL in your browser. Paste the authorization code from the browser into the running command and press Enter when prompted.\\n00103| - **Codex**. Enable device code sign-in in your ChatGPT security settings or workspace permissions. Open the supplied URL and enter the displayed code in your browser. The command waits for approval. [Codex authentication](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/auth?surface=cli#login-on-headless-devices)\\n00104| - **GitHub**. Enter the displayed one-time code at [GitHub device activation](https://github.com/login/device). The command also sets up the Git identity and HTTPS credentials in the target environment.\\n00105| \\n00106| Append `status` to any sign-in command to check the selected environment. Use a private terminal when working with one-time codes. For provider-native commands and deployments without the manager, see the [manual operations guide](hive-manager-manual.md).\\n00107| \\n00108| ## Updates and recovery\\n00109| \\n00110| ```bash\\n00111| hive-manager deploy --profile ./production.conf doctor\\n00112| hive-manager deploy --profile ./production.conf prepare\\n00113| hive-manager deploy --profile ./production.conf update\\n00114| hive-manager deploy --profile ./production.conf rollback\\n00115| ```\\n00116| \\n00117| Keep the same profile for every update. The manager pulls and validates the selected image and checks Telegram configuration before stopping the previous container. It restores the previous container if startup fails and retains one stopped rollback container after a successful replacement. Readiness is based on the image health check when present and otherwise on the container staying running across consecutive checks. Inspect the running bot's logs for service-level problems. Rollback switches containers while keeping shared state as it exists at the time of rollback. Run `deploy recover` after an interrupted rollout. It handles containers left at the `.candidate` and `.previous` stages.\\n00118| \\n00119| To bring an existing Docker deployment under management, identify its image variant, state mounts, configuration, and startup command. Back up its state and point the profile at the same state directory if it uses the documented `.hive-mind` layout. Copy any state stored only in the old container before replacing it. Run `hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE --adopt --dry-run apply` to check the configuration and remove `--dry-run` to proceed. The manager records the original container ID for subsequent rollbacks and updates. Set up GitHub credentials with `hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE auth github` if they need to be recreated. Use Compose to preserve custom volumes or mounts.\\n00120| \\n00121| ## Maintenance and diagnostics\\n00122| \\n00123| Each package operation is available locally and inside a managed deployment. Use the command's `--help` for its options.\\n00124| \\n00125| | Purpose                   | Current shell                             | Managed instance                                 |\\n00126| | ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\\n00127| | Preview disk cleanup      | `hive-manager cleanup --dry-run`          | `hive-manager deploy cleanup --dry-run`          |\\n00128| | Verify Claude settings    | `hive-manager configure-claude --verify`  | `hive-manager deploy configure-claude --verify`  |\\n00129| | Audit agent settings      | `hive-manager audit-config`               | `hive-manager deploy audit-config`               |\\n00130| | List Screen sessions      | `hive-manager screens --list`             | `hive-manager deploy screens --list`             |\\n00131| | Inspect the model catalog | `hive-manager models --tool codex --json` | `hive-manager deploy models --tool codex --json` |\\n00132| | Reconcile linked issues   | `hive-manager close-linked-issues --help` | `hive-manager deploy close-linked-issues --help` |\\n00133| | Inspect task records      | `hive-manager tasks list --running`       | `hive-manager deploy tasks list --running`       |\\n00134| \\n00135| `deploy status` summarizes the selected instance, `deploy logs` accepts Docker log options, and `deploy doctor` checks free space, Docker, and the package runtime. Use `deploy disk` for bounded host and task storage accounting and `deploy diagnose` for resource usage, exit status and OOM information. `deploy start`, `stop` and `restart` manage the selected container. On systemd hosts, `deploy docker-service status` checks the host daemon and `deploy docker-service start` starts it. `deploy reboot --confirm` requests a systemd reboot of the host and requires host privileges. `audit-config --repair` applies Hive Mind's existing global agent settings repair and quarantines supported non-baseline additions.\\n00136| \\n00137| ### Scoped tool maintenance\\n00138| \\n00139| Each action previews by default and writes a JSON receipt to stdout. Save the receipt if a scheduler or an incident report needs it. Add `--apply` after the action once you have reviewed the plan.\\n00140| \\n00141| ```bash\\n00142| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE update-dependencies gh-upload-log\\n00143| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE update-dependencies --apply gh-upload-log\\n00144| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE update-dependencies --all\\n00145| ```\\n00146| \\n00147| The dependency command runs Bun in the selected deployment as `box`. It uses `BUN_INSTALL_GLOBAL_DIR` when set, or the global install directory under `BUN_INSTALL` or `~/.bun`. The preview shows Bun's proposed versions. Apply backs up the original `package.json`, `bun.lock` and `bun.lockb` when present under `~/.hive-mind/state/tool-maintenance/dependency-backups/` before invoking Bun. The receipt includes the backup path, before and after versions and file checksums, and Bun output. A failed update retains its backup. To roll it back, restore the saved manifest and lockfile format in the original Bun global directory, remove any lockfile generated in the failed update whose original checksum was null, and reinstall from the restored manifest and lockfile. Keep this directory on the deployment's persistent state mount.\\n00148| \\n00149| Run the other two actions as root on the Linux Docker host using an installed Hive Mind Manager and Python 3.11 or later. For SSH, select an authorized root destination with `--host`. `tool-services` also requires Linux pidfd support.\\n00150| \\n00151| ```bash\\n00152| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE global-tools --policy /etc/hive-mind/stage-tools.json\\n00153| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE global-tools --policy /etc/hive-mind/stage-tools.json --apply\\n00154| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE tool-services --tools codex,claude\\n00155| hive-manager deploy --profile PROFILE tool-services --tools codex,claude --apply\\n00156| ```\\n00157| \\n00158| Store the allowlist on the Docker host. A deployment profile can supply `tool-policy=stage-tools.json` relative to that profile. Each of the `codexMcp` and `claudeMcp` objects maps an approved name to its exact registration in that tool's format. For instance, this file approves Playwright for both tools using the published Control Center arguments.\\n00159| \\n00160| ```json\\n00161| {\\n00162|   \\\"codexMcp\\\": {\\n00163|     \\\"playwright\\\": {\\n00164|       \\\"command\\\": \\\"npx\\\",\\n00165|       \\\"args\\\": [\\\"-y\\\", \\\"@playwright/mcp@latest\\\", \\\"--isolated\\\", \\\"--headless\\\", \\\"--no-sandbox\\\", \\\"--timeout-action=600000\\\", \\\"--viewport-size\\\", \\\"1920x1080\\\"]\\n00166|     }\\n00167|   },\\n00168|   \\\"claudeMcp\\\": {\\n00169|     \\\"playwright\\\": {\\n00170|       \\\"type\\\": \\\"stdio\\\",\\n00171|       \\\"command\\\": \\\"npx\\\",\\n00172|       \\\"args\\\": [\\\"-y\\\", \\\"@playwright/mcp@latest\\\", \\\"--isolated\\\", \\\"--headless\\\", \\\"--no-sandbox\\\", \\\"--timeout-action=600000\\\", \\\"--viewport-size\\\", \\\"1920x1080\\\"]\\n00173|     }\\n00174|   }\\n00175| }\\n00176| ```\\n00177| \\n00178| Use `{}` for an empty allowlist. A deployment running the Hive Mind task-launch policy must also satisfy that policy before admitting tasks. On a shared host, align the policy across deployments for the shared root and system layers. The audit covers the bot user, bot root and host root homes, and managed Codex and Claude configuration on the host and in the deployment. It preserves stock Codex system skills, credentials, session history, task-scoped settings and managed permission constraints. Apply records original configurations and moved extensions in root-only quarantine under `/var/lib/hive-manager/tool-maintenance/quarantine/`. Single-file bind-mounted configs are rewritten in place. An audit exits nonzero on drift or an unsafe configuration. An apply exits nonzero if violations remain.\\n00179| \\n00180| Service cleanup selects the deployment's known managed Codex app-server, its updater, and orphaned Claude browser helpers. Add `--include-host` to consider eligible helpers in the host scope as well. The receipt lists blockers such as active or unclassified tasks, foreground tool processes, controlling terminals, owners and worker children. It reads the current start-command store without reconciling tasks or refreshing its cache. Each signal checks the current deployment identity, task state and process incarnation. Apply stops the updater first, sends SIGTERM to eligible helpers, and rechecks before escalating to SIGKILL if necessary.\\n00181| \\n00182| Control Center schedulers can switch their focused dependency, global-tool and service maintenance calls to these commands once the deployment and its host package run the supporting release. Compare each JSON preview with the existing runbook on staging, then verify the backups, preserved scopes and service blockers before switching callers. A pinned image rollout restores the image's pinned dependencies. The [queue work classifier](../../tests/queue-work-accounting-4021.test.mjs) discounts idle daemons for queue admission.\\n00183| \\n00184| `tasks status ID` reports one execution. `tasks report` tallies execution states and lists recent failures with exit and memory evidence in JSON. Use `tasks attach ID --read-only` to follow a live task, `tasks resume ID` to restart the stored execution, or `tasks resume-all` to reconcile running records and watchers. `tasks cleanup-dry-run [ID]` previews stale record or task container cleanup and `tasks cleanup [ID]` performs it. `tasks alias` and `tasks rename` manage task names. `tasks upload-log ID` invokes the sanitized upload workflow. The installed [start-command](https://github.com/link-foundation/start) version determines which actions and arguments are supported.\\n00185| \\n00186| To preserve a failed Docker task before removing its container, run `tasks backup-container CONTAINER --output NEW_DIRECTORY`, then preview removal with `tasks cleanup-backed-up NEW_DIRECTORY`. Supply `--apply SHA256` with the preview’s approval digest to remove the exact stopped container after rechecking the backup checksum and container identity. For an older container without task labels, add `--execution UUID` to the backup command. The manager checks that the stored Docker task recorded the same container, session and exit code. With `deploy tasks`, choose a directory on a persistent mount with enough free space. The private backup is a flattened Docker filesystem export. Inspect it with `tar -tzf NEW_DIRECTORY/filesystem.tar.gz`. Preserve mounted data and task logs separately. [Docker export](https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/export/)\\n00187| \\n00188| ### Host storage reporting and cleanup\\n00189| \\n00190| `hive-manager deploy disk` reports filesystem capacity and inodes, Docker images and volumes, selected daemon metadata and host directories, and individual writable layers and Docker logs. It includes paused and stopped tasks. Pass `--name` and the deployment profile before `disk` to match Docker containers to the bot's saved start-command records and issue or pull request URLs. The report reads the execution store directly, with a 16 MiB read limit. Use `--budget SECONDS` to set the total measurement budget (60 seconds by default, up to 180). The JSON lists unavailable measurements as `null` and explains them in `issues`. Run on the Linux daemon host with read access to its storage for layer and volume measurements. Use `disk --docker-summary` for Docker's separate verbose storage walk.\\n00191| \\n00192| Run scoped cleanup as root on the Linux daemon host. It uses Node.js 24+, a local Docker Unix socket and `flock`. A task needs Node.js 24+, a verified overlay writable layer, and a verifiable cgroup v2 freezer for removal. The worker verifies the pinned disk-space-saviour archive before scanning and does one scope at a time.\\n00193| \\n00194| ```sh\\n00195| hive-manager deploy cleanup --scoped --dry-run\\n00196| hive-manager deploy cleanup --scoped --status\\n00197| hive-manager deploy cleanup --scoped --apply PREVIEW_ID\\n00198| hive-manager deploy cleanup --scoped --status\\n00199| ```\\n00200| \\n00201| The default is a preview. Review the completed status and the recorded `previewFile` before applying. Apply rescans and considers only approved files that still match the preview. It skips paused and stopped tasks, mounted data, Docker and containerd storage, active files detected by the scanner, and files that only exist in the image. It leaves directory ownership intact. The host worker holds a lock and records status under `/var/lib/hive-manager/scoped-cleanup`. Reconnect and check `--status` after an SSH interruption. A pressure stop, interrupted worker, or partial apply requires a fresh preview. Limits include 1,536 MiB of available host memory, 2,560 MiB maximum Docker daemon RSS, and 256 MiB of headroom in a bounded worker or daemon cgroup. Inspect each scope's outcome in the status before relying on its estimates.\\n00202| \\n00203| ### Failed task preservation\\n00204| \\n00205| For stopped overlay task containers on the daemon host, use `hive-manager deploy tasks preserve-layer CONTAINER --output NEW_DIRECTORY` as root. Supply `--execution UUID` for an older unlabelled container. The private receipt covers the native writable-layer tar with numeric owners, modes, ACLs and extended attributes, original container facts, the matching start-command record and task log, and retained Docker stdout and stderr. Save mounted data independently using the mount inventory in `container.json`. Keep the original image if you need to interpret the layer over its base filesystem. A layer archive can contain whiteouts and overlay metadata. It needs an overlay-aware restore procedure.\\n00206| \\n00207| Run `hive-manager deploy tasks cleanup-preserved NEW_DIRECTORY` to verify the stored files and check the live container identity. Pass `--apply SHA256` with the preview's approval digest to remove only that stopped container. Use `tasks backup-container` when you need a flattened filesystem export.\\n00208| \\n00209| `tasks recover-failed` previews first retries for detached Docker tasks with confirmed memory exhaustion. It skips active or paused families, tasks still tracked by the Telegram bot, operator stops recorded by start-command, and failures superseded by newer successes. Let the bot reconcile after a restart and use `tasks status UUID` to check the latest attempt before applying a plan.\\n00210| \\n00211| Select a known Telegram solve alias that exited 127 or a later confirmed memory failure by execution UUID and failed attempt number. Choose `--max-resumes` to match the deployment policy. The default is 1, and the maximum is 10. Recorded automatic restarts count toward the cap. These commands review a second attempt and allow one more retry.\\n00212| \\n00213| ```bash\\n00214| hive-manager tasks status UUID\\n00215| hive-manager tasks recover-failed --execution UUID --attempt 2 --max-resumes 2\\n00216| hive-manager tasks recover-failed --execution UUID --attempt 2 --max-resumes 2 --apply --wait 60\\n00217| ```\\n00218| \\n00219| The `snapshot` result shows the stopped container's writable-layer bytes, required bytes (twice the layer size plus 10 GiB), and the lowest available bytes across the Docker image-store filesystems. Run recovery where the local Docker daemon's storage paths are visible, using `deploy tasks` when the daemon runs inside the deployment. `--apply` rechecks the attempt, container, and headroom immediately before handing off to start-command. An alias recovery launches `solve` with the stored arguments and alias tool selection. If the attempt log has a tool session ID in its bounded tail, it replaces any stale `--resume` argument. `--wait SECONDS` tracks the specific attempt launched, and a blocked, failed, or unfinished retry makes the command exit nonzero.\\n00220| \\n00221| Keep the deployment's [Control Center recovery and reporting runbook](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind-control-center/issues/1) for historical records that still need operator review. Check the native previews and `tasks status` before taking action on these records.\\n00222| \\n00223| `tasks check-uploads` samples same-user Linux upload and sanitization processes. The defaults flag processes at least 30 minutes old, averaging at least 80% of one CPU over three seconds, with no observed I/O progress. `--terminate PID` signals the selected process after rechecking its identity and progress.\\n00224| \\n00225| Run `tasks repair-records [UUID]` when a detached Docker resume is running and its stored execution still shows an older terminal attempt. The preview checks the exact labels, start timestamp, snapshot and original command. Apply with `--apply` to adopt an eligible snapshot and attach a completion watcher. This repair uses the store format from the image's pinned start-command 0.44.0. Use `tasks resume-all` for records already marked executing.\\n00226| \\n00227| `tasks report --group` counts recurring failures across recorded attempts, including failures from tasks that later succeeded. It groups by execution backend, failure classification and exit code. `tasks report --incident UUID --attempt NUMBER --destination OWNER/REPO` previews an incident for one confirmed failure. The default attempt is the latest. Add `--follow-up ISSUE_NUMBER` when the failure belongs on an existing open issue. Use `--publish REVIEW_DIGEST` after reviewing the JSON output to create the issue or post the approved comment. The report contains the execution ID, times, backend, exit status and classification. Upload an execution log separately with `tasks upload-log ID` if needed.\\n00228| \\n00229| `tasks report --group --by-signature` also groups by known solver outcomes, such as an empty repository, a dirty checkout, an exhausted restart budget or a Codex output limit. It reads up to 512 KiB from the end of each attempt and returns groups without a signature when a cause cannot be established from the available evidence.\\n00230| \\n00231| `tasks report --collect UUID [--attempt NUMBER]` inspects up to 4 MiB of the selected attempt's log. It reports short success runs, retry and reconnection counts, and repeated commands, MCP calls or messages. The result contains counts and classifications without raw log lines. Historical collection needs stored log offsets to distinguish one attempt from the next.\\n00232| \\n00233| For repeat failures, use `--routes /path/to/routes.json` in place of `--follow-up`. The file is read in the environment that runs the command, so put it inside the container when using `deploy tasks`. Copy the `backend`, `kind` and `exitCode` values from `tasks report --group` and add the issue number in the selected destination repository. To route to a cause-specific issue, also copy the optional `signature` from `tasks report --group --by-signature`.\\n00234| \\n00235| ```json\\n00236| [{ \\\"backend\\\": \\\"docker\\\", \\\"kind\\\": \\\"Memory exhaustion\\\", \\\"exitCode\\\": 137, \\\"issue\\\": 318 }]\\n00237| ```\\n00238| \\n00239| A failure routed to an open issue previews a comment. An unmatched failure previews a new issue. An optional `signature` from `--collect`, such as `codex-oom` or `stream-close-timeout`, takes precedence over a group-only match. For a signal-range Docker exit, add `--host-signature NAME` after reviewing the corresponding attempt's journal correlation on the Docker host. The report includes the selected correlation and can route by that signature. Changes to the selected issue or its preview evidence after review invalidate the digest.\\n00240| \\n00241| Use `--reconcile` to review historical reports. It checks the exact execution and attempt markers in GitHub issues and comments for the selected repository. Supply `--legacy-ledger FILE` to check a Control Center `auto-fix-reports.json` export as well. A reconciliation preview has no publication digest.\\n00242| \\n00243| ```bash\\n00244| hive-manager tasks report --incident UUID --attempt 2 --destination OWNER/REPO \\\\\\n00245|   --routes /path/to/routes.json --reconcile --legacy-ledger /path/to/auto-fix-reports.json\\n00246| ```\\n00247| \\n00248| For a closed route, the preview links the issue and compares its close time with the installed Hive Mind release's publication time. Use `--installed-version VERSION` when reviewing a deployment that runs a different release. A closure after that version was released indicates that the fix may still need to be deployed. A closure before or at release time marks a possible regression for review. Use the legacy incident procedure for those records and use `tasks report --incident` with a reviewed digest for newly verified failures. Publication returns the existing GitHub report URL when the attempt has already been reported.\\n00249| \\n00250| After a Telegram bot restart, save the previous and current verbose logs locally. Use `deploy logs --timestamps` with `--until` and `--since` around the restart. Run `tasks recover-queue --previous previous.log --current current.log` to list requests from the last pre-restart run that never reached launch, excluding requests the current run already queued or began launching. Both files need their queue initialization line. `--include-commands` includes matching raw Telegram text for local review when available. Once the bot has reconciled, the deployment operator checks each candidate against the live queue and task records, confirms its dependencies and authorized chat with the request owner, and resubmits it once through that chat.\\n00251| \\n00252| On a Linux Docker host, `deploy docker-service live-restore` checks whether the local daemon keeps containers running when the daemon is unavailable. Run it with `--apply` as root on a systemd host with `jq` and `flock`. The command validates and backs up `/etc/docker/daemon.json`, reloads `docker.service`, and checks the running daemon. It restores the previous file if the setting fails to take effect. [Docker live restore](https://docs.docker.com/engine/daemon/live-restore/)\\n00253| \\n00254| Use `deploy diagnose --since '1 hour ago'` to include bounded Docker service and kernel memory-failure history where the target account can read the systemd journal. The journal remains available when the managed container or its daemon is down. Use `deploy diagnose --task UUID --attempt NUMBER` on a running instance to use the selected task's recorded time window for both journal queries. The results show events overlapping the task, with up to 80 records per query. Near a completed attempt's exit, the command correlates a recorded Docker stop and start as `daemon-restart`. With `jq` and a retained journal from the previous boot, it can identify a `host-reboot` when shutdown is recorded or a possible `host-reset` when the journal ends abruptly. Review the events and the completeness of the journal before using a correlation in an incident. On a running Linux instance, `deploy diagnose --resources` adds cgroup v2 memory, CPU, pressure and PID counters, thread wait channels, a bounded browser process table, and shared-memory usage where the Docker host exposes them.\\n00255| \\n00256| To inspect a blocked browser or agent process, get its container PID with `deploy exec -- ps -eo pid,comm` and run `deploy diagnose --process PID`. The command samples read and write byte and operation counters, the current syscall number and queued bytes for connected Unix stream sockets at both ends of the sample. It runs as `box` within the selected container. Socket queue sampling uses the Linux kernel diagnostic interface through Python 3. The command identifies readings that the kernel or container makes unavailable. For a separate Docker task container, select that container with `deploy --name TASK_CONTAINER diagnose --process PID`.\\n00257| \\n00258| Run `tasks check-browser PID` as the browser's user in the same Linux process namespace to sample a headless Chromium tree. For a task container on the selected Docker daemon, use `deploy --name TASK_CONTAINER tasks check-browser PID`. To inspect a child of a Docker-in-Docker deployment, use `deploy exec -- docker exec --user box TASK_CONTAINER hive-manager tasks check-browser PID`. Add `--transcript SESSION.jsonl` to check the corresponding Claude session for an idle, CPU-intensive Playwright browser, including recent calls from subagents. Approval requires complete, bounded transcripts. `--detached` checks whether init adopted the browser. Review the reported activity and pass `--terminate sha256:HEX` with the approval shown for the selected browser. The manager samples again and sends SIGTERM when the process and evidence still match. Closing an idle browser discards its open page.\\n00259| \\n00260| Packaged maintenance commands use Hive Mind's terminal credential filter. Operations such as `deploy logs`, `deploy exec`, and backup streams carry the underlying service output, so review that output before sharing it.\\n00261| \\n00262| `archive-session FILE` writes a sanitized local copy to stdout. `archive-sessions --repo OWNER/REPO --output NEW_DIRECTORY` discovers inactive Claude and Codex sessions, verifies the repository is public, and stages sanitized files and a manifest. It uses Codex session metadata and Claude checkout origins to attribute sessions. A Claude subagent without its own working directory inherits its parent’s repository. Use `--only claude` or `--only codex` to export one format. The default export limit is 20. Use `--min-age MINUTES` to carry over the scheduler's idle threshold for transcripts and attachments. The default is 10 minutes since modification. Set a shorter threshold only when the operator can ensure the session has ended. The manager reports attributed inactive transcripts above 40 MiB by tool on stderr and in `manifest.json` under `omissions.oversized`, including when the export is empty. Those transcripts need a separate reviewed export. Add `--lino` with the [Conversation CLI](https://github.com/link-assistant/conversation/tree/e548823507862f237ce58cb2915bf336f29a725c) installed to add Links Notation copies. Session files can include personal or proprietary material. Review the staged files before publishing.\\n00263| \\n00264| Use `--tool-results` to include sanitized Claude attachments associated with the staged transcripts. This selects inactive, regular `.txt`, `.log`, `.json`, and `.jsonl` files up to 4 MiB each, with a shared limit of 100 attachments or 40 MiB of input per export.\\n00265| \\n00266| ```bash\\n00267| ( umask 077; hive-manager --host hive-production deploy archive-session /home/box/.claude/projects/example/session.jsonl > session.redacted.jsonl )\\n00268| hive-manager archive-sessions --repo link-assistant/hive-mind --output ./session-review --tool-results\\n00269| ```\\n00270| \\n00271| `archive-publish` lists the exact files to be committed and returns a review digest tied to the destination's visibility and default branch. After reviewing every file, pass the digest with `--apply` to publish one normal Git commit. The destination must already contain a default branch and allow your configured Git identity and credentials to push to it. The manager rechecks source visibility and scans the reviewed files before pushing. It redacts recognizable identity-document details while staging and excludes the repository identified in the Control Center's known data exposure. E
[2026-11-23T08:04:48.493Z] [INFO] ach publication accepts up to 256 MiB total and 64 MiB per file.\\n00272| \\n00273| ```bash\\n00274| hive-manager archive-publish ./session-review --destination example/session-archive\\n00275| hive-manager archive-publish ./session-review --destination example/session-archive --apply REVIEW_DIGEST\\n00276| ```\\n00277| \\n00278| To create a destination with an initial branch and README, run `hive-manager archive-publish --initialize --destination example/session-archive --visibility private` with GitHub CLI credentials that can create repositories. Choose `public` or `internal` explicitly when required.\\n00279| \\n00280| Use `archive-sessions --published-in example/session-archive` when working through a backlog or staging batches on a schedule. The manager reads the destination's Git index for this source without downloading existing session logs. It skips an already published transcript revision with the same export options and selected attachments, so the next batch can advance through the backlog. A changed transcript or attachment is eligible again. The Git commit publishes the revision index and its reviewed archive together.\\n00281| \\n00282| For a scheduler such as cron or a systemd timer, use `archive-sessions --repo example/project --publish-private example/session-archive`. It stages a batch of up to three new revisions by default, scans them for residual credentials and identity data, verifies that the destination remains private before pushing, then removes the temporary files. Run it as the account that owns the session directories and has Git and GitHub access to the destination. Use a separate scheduled invocation for each source repository you intend to archive. Existing archive files stay in place when using an existing destination. The manager counts revisions published through its own index.\\n00283| \\n00284| Pass `--known-secrets-file /path/to/private-literals.txt` to `archive-sessions` for source-specific redactions. Carry over the literals configured for that source in the scheduler and keep the file outside the archive, readable only by the scheduler account. Use one UTF-8 literal per line. Surrounding whitespace and blank lines are ignored, and regex characters match literally. The same literal set is required for `archive-publish` preview and apply of a staged batch that uses it. Both reviewed and scheduled publication require a private destination when extra literals are supplied. A match anywhere in the final artifacts, including a converted file, blocks publication.\\n00285| \\n00286| ### Import an existing private archive\\n00287| \\n00288| Pause the archive scheduler and record the current destination commit. Run the import with the same source roots, `--only`, `--lino`, `--tool-results`, `--min-age` and `--known-secrets-file` settings you intend to use for the incremental export. For example:\\n00289| \\n00290| ```bash\\n00291| hive-manager archive-sessions --repo example/project --import-legacy example/session-archive --lino --known-secrets-file /path/to/private-literals.txt\\n00292| hive-manager archive-sessions --repo example/project --import-legacy example/session-archive --lino --known-secrets-file /path/to/private-literals.txt --apply REVIEW_DIGEST\\n00293| hive-manager archive-sessions --repo example/project --published-in example/session-archive --output ./first-increment --lino --known-secrets-file /path/to/private-literals.txt\\n00294| hive-manager archive-publish ./first-increment --destination example/session-archive --private --known-secrets-file /path/to/private-literals.txt\\n00295| ```\\n00296| \\n00297| The import preview lists the matched legacy metadata and corresponding revision markers, skipped candidates by reason, and the destination commit bound to the review digest. Inspect the listed legacy output before approving it as the baseline. The import verifies the transcript SHA-256 against the inactive source, checks the declared archived files and their checksums, and verifies that the requested Links Notation and attachment choices match the available evidence. Each selected Claude attachment needs its own matching source hash and archived file. Split artifacts, missing or changed evidence, ambiguous attachment policies, and archived artifacts containing configured literals remain eligible for a fresh export under the selected options and size limit. Changing the Links Notation option, attachment selection, or extra literals produces a distinct revision. Use `--limit` to bound each import, and repeat preview and apply until the eligible index batch is empty.\\n00298| \\n00299| Apply adds only revision markers in a normal Git commit on the existing private branch. If the destination or plan changed, generate a fresh preview. Review and apply the first incremental batch with `archive-publish --private` before resuming the scheduler. Keep the archive private because its history remains intact. To roll back an import, pause the scheduler and revert the import commit on the existing branch. The manager preserves the legacy README as a historical inventory. New batches record their inventory in their own `manifest.json`, and deployments that need a regenerated overview can build it from those manifests alongside the historical README.\\n00300| \\n00301| ## Backup and restore\\n00302| \\n00303| Stop the instance first if an application-consistent snapshot is needed. Run backup with permission to read the state files. Files can be owned by the container user.\\n00304| \\n00305| ```bash\\n00306| hive-manager deploy --profile ./production.conf backup --output ./hive-state.tar.gz\\n00307| hive-manager deploy --state-dir ./restored-state restore --input ./hive-state.tar.gz\\n00308| \\n00309| ( umask 077; hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --profile ./hive/production.conf backup --output - > hive-state.tar.gz )\\n00310| hive-manager --host replacement deploy --state-dir ./restored-state restore < hive-state.tar.gz\\n00311| ```\\n00312| \\n00313| The backup is a private archive of the state directory. Keep configuration inside that directory if it should be included, and preserve separate files and Docker volumes independently. The state directory includes Claude, Codex, Agent skills, Git, GitHub CLI, Hive Mind session state, and start-command records and logs. Archives can contain credentials. Restore a trusted archive into a new directory and ensure its files are accessible by the `box` user before applying the profile.\\n00314| \\n00315| ## Telegram MTProto proxy\\n00316| \\n00317| The proxy can run independently of the bot. Use `apply` to create or start a managed classic proxy. Use `deploy --adopt proxy --proxy-name NAME apply` to recreate a legacy classic proxy with the manager ownership label. When the legacy container has a classic `/data` bind mount, the manager reads its key. You can also supply a private `--secret-file`. A supplied key must agree with the mounted key and any saved manager key. The legacy container and its configuration stay available for rollback.\\n00318| \\n00319| ```bash\\n00320| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy proxy --port 8443 apply\\n00321| hive-manager --host hive-production deploy proxy --port 8443 verify\\n00322| hive-manager deploy proxy --port 8443 --public-host proxy.example.net connect\\n00323| ```\\n00324| \\n00325| `verify` checks running status, the selected binding and TCP connectivity to each Telegram data center from the selected container's network namespace. Run it on the Linux Docker host with permission to enter Docker namespaces. A connection failure is reported as `unreachable`. A missing namespace, inaccessible namespace or nonlocal Docker endpoint is reported as `unavailable`. Both cause verification to fail. `connect` checks public TCP reachability from the caller.\\n00326| \\n00327| Use `snapshot` to record the image, restart policy, workers, published bindings and a SHA-256 hash of the supplied or persisted key. Add `--check-egress` and `--check-mss` to include namespace connectivity and installed IPv4 TCPMSS rules. With `--secret-file`, snapshot and compare can read the legacy key in place. The path is on the Docker host. `compare` accepts a baseline on the target or `-` for stdin over SSH.\\n00328| \\n00329| ```bash\\n00330| proxy=(hive-manager --host hive-production deploy proxy --port 8443 --proxy-name mtproxy-classic-8443)\\n00331| observations=(--secret-file /ro…ret --check-egress --check-mss)\\n00332| \\\"${proxy[@]}\\\" \\\"${observations[@]}\\\" snapshot > proxy.baseline\\n00333| \\\"${proxy[@]}\\\" \\\"${observations[@]}\\\" compare - < proxy.baseline\\n00334| ```\\n00335| \\n00336| Run `proxy-test` on the affected client network with a pre-authorized Telegram StringSession and private API credentials. It verifies a text round trip in Saved Messages and deletes its test messages. `--media` adds a byte-for-byte document round trip. `--roundtrip-photo` uploads a deterministic 128 × 128 test image. `--photo FILE` accepts a selected JPEG or PNG of at most 256 KiB. Both options compare decoded dimensions and pixels with a tolerance for recompression. Use `--direct` to run the same checks without a proxy.\\n00337| \\n00338| ```bash\\n00339| hive-manager proxy-test --credentials [REDACTED] --session-file ./telegram.session \\\\\\n00340|   --proxy-host proxy.example.net --proxy-port 8443 --secret-file [REDACTED] \\\\\\n00341|   --media --roundtrip-photo\\n00342| ```\\n00343| \\n00344| ### Compare routes on the same client network\\n00345| \\n00346| `--matrix routes.json` runs the selected tests sequentially against existing routes. Store secret files relative to the matrix, including the hexadecimal FakeTLS key if applicable. When multiple candidates share a port, run one matrix entry at a time with `--route NAME` after activating the matching candidate.\\n00347| \\n00348| ```json\\n00349| [\\n00350|   { \\\"name\\\": \\\"direct\\\", \\\"direct\\\": true },\\n00351|   { \\\"name\\\": \\\"baseline\\\", \\\"host\\\": \\\"proxy.example.net\\\", \\\"port\\\": 8443, \\\"secretFile\\\": \\\"baseline.secret\\\" },\\n00352|   { \\\"name\\\": \\\"candidate\\\", \\\"host\\\": \\\"proxy.example.net\\\", \\\"port\\\": 8443, \\\"secretFile\\\": \\\"candidate.secret\\\" }\\n00353| ]\\n00354| ```\\n00355| \\n00356| Run the baseline client probe first. For a managed classic configuration change, `proxy update` activates the new image and/or worker setting and retains the predecessor. For other configurations, such as FakeTLS, create a stopped candidate with `docker create`, `--restart unless-stopped`, and the same selected host port mapped to TCP 443, using its own reviewed configuration. Review the two container IDs, image IDs and bindings before applying the plan. Use the same target, deployment name, state directory, port and proxy name throughout the rollout.\\n00357| \\n00358| ```bash\\n00359| rollout=(hive-manager --host hive-production deploy --adopt proxy --port 8443 --proxy-name mtproxy-classic-8443)\\n00360| trial=(--candidate staged-faketls)\\n00361| \\\"${rollout[@]}\\\" \\\"${trial[@]}\\\" trial-plan > trial.plan\\n00362| cat trial.plan\\n00363| \\\"${rollout[@]}\\\" \\\"${trial[@]}\\\" trial - < trial.plan\\n00364| ```\\n00365| \\n00366| Run the candidate client probe with the same account, client network and media options. Save each `proxy-test` JSON result. Use the same route name across reports if you want numeric deltas for that route. To restore the predecessor, run `\\\"${rollout[@]}\\\" rollback`. Rollback restores its original running or stopped state and returns a staged candidate to its original name for another run. If activation is interrupted, use the same rollback command to restore the recorded predecessor. After the selected candidate passes the live text, document and photo checks, run `\\\"${rollout[@]}\\\" accept` to remove the retained predecessor. Keep the local data directories and image if needed for subsequent trials.\\n00367| \\n00368| ### Capture and compare TCP and application results\\n00369| \\n00370| Collect bounded classic pcap files on both external interfaces during the same probe window. `proxy-pcap` reports payload bytes, retransmitted segments and bytes, duplicate ACKs, resets and per-direction ACK gaps. Its comparable flow count shows where both payload and peer ACKs were observed. Interface offloading and capture loss can affect the measurements. Attach the `proxy-test` JSON with `--probe` to record the application outcome alongside the packet counts. `--compare` compares two saved reports or two standalone probe results.\\n00371| \\n00372| ```bash\\n00373| ( umask 077; hive-manager --host hive-production deploy proxy --port 8443 --interface eth0 --seconds 15 --client-ip 192.0.2.12 capture > server.pcap )\\n00374| hive-manager proxy-pcap --port 8443 --probe candidate-probe.json client.pcap server.pcap > candidate.json\\n00375| hive-manager proxy-pcap --compare baseline.json candidate.json\\n00376| ```\\n00377| \\n00378| ### Stop or remove legacy proxy resources\\n00379| \\n\\n(Output capped at 50 KB. Showing lines 1-379. Use offset=379 to continue.)\\n</file>\"}],\"tools\":[{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"description\":\"Executes a given bash command in a persistent shell session with optional timeout, ensuring proper handling and security measures.\\n\\nBefore executing the command, please follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Directory Verification:\\n   - If the command will create new directories or files, first use the List tool to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location\\n   - For example, before running \\\"mkdir foo/bar\\\", first use List to check that \\\"foo\\\" exists and is the intended parent directory\\n\\n2. Command Execution:\\n   - Always quote file paths that contain spaces with double quotes (e.g., cd \\\"path with spaces/file.txt\\\")\\n   - Examples of proper quoting:\\n     - cd \\\"/Users/name/My Documents\\\" (correct)\\n     - cd /Users/name/My Documents (incorrect - will fail)\\n     - python \\\"/path/with spaces/script.py\\\" (correct)\\n     - python /path/with spaces/script.py (incorrect - will fail)\\n   - After ensuring proper quoting, execute the command.\\n   - Capture the output of the command.\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - The command argument is required.\\n  - You can specify an optional timeout in milliseconds (up to 600000ms / 10 minutes). If not specified, commands will timeout after 120000ms (2 minutes).\\n  - It is very helpful if you write a clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words.\\n  - If the output exceeds 2000 lines or 30000 bytes, the beginning of the output is dropped and only the end, where errors usually are, is returned to you.\\n  - VERY IMPORTANT: You MUST avoid using search commands like `find` and `grep`. Instead use Grep, Glob, or Task to search. You MUST avoid read tools like `cat`, `head`, `tail`, and `ls`, and use Read and List to read files.\\n  - If you _still_ need to run `grep`, STOP. ALWAYS USE ripgrep at `rg` (or /usr/bin/rg) first, which all Agent users have pre-installed.\\n  - When issuing multiple commands, use the ';' or '&&' operator to separate them. DO NOT use newlines (newlines are ok in quoted strings).\\n  - Try to maintain your current working directory throughout the session by using absolute paths and avoiding usage of `cd`. You may use `cd` if the User explicitly requests it.\\n    <good-example>\\n    pytest /foo/bar/tests\\n    </good-example>\\n    <bad-example>\\n    cd /foo/bar && pytest tests\\n    </bad-example>\\n\\n\\n# Committing changes with git\\n\\nWhen the user asks you to create a new git commit, follow these steps carefully:\\n\\n1. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following bash commands in parallel, each using the Bash tool:\\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files.\\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed.\\n   - Run a git log command to see recent commit messages, so that you can follow this repository's commit message style.\\n\\n2. Analyze all staged changes (both previously staged and newly added) and draft a commit message. Wrap your analysis process in <commit_analysis> tags:\\n\\n<commit_analysis>\\n- List the files that have been changed or added\\n- Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)\\n- Brainstorm the purpose or motivation behind these changes\\n- Assess the impact of these changes on the overall project\\n- Check for any sensitive information that shouldn't be committed\\n- Draft a concise (1-2 sentences) commit message that focuses on the \\\"why\\\" rather than the \\\"what\\\"\\n- Ensure your language is clear, concise, and to the point\\n- Ensure the message accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (i.e. \\\"add\\\" means a wholly new feature, \\\"update\\\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \\\"fix\\\" means a bug fix, etc.)\\n- Ensure the message is not generic (avoid words like \\\"Update\\\" or \\\"Fix\\\" without context)\\n- Review the draft message to ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\\n</commit_analysis>\\n\\n3. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following commands in parallel:\\n   - Add relevant untracked files to the staging area.\\n   - Run git status to make sure the commit succeeded.\\n\\n4. If the commit fails due to pre-commit hook changes, retry the commit ONCE to include these automated changes. If it fails again, it usually means a pre-commit hook is preventing the commit. If the commit succeeds but you notice that files were modified by the pre-commit hook, you MUST amend your commit to include them.\\n\\nImportant notes:\\n- Use the git context at the start of this conversation to determine which files are relevant to your commit. Be careful not to stage and commit files (e.g. with `git add .`) that aren't relevant to your commit.\\n- NEVER update the git config\\n- DO NOT run additional commands to read or explore code, beyond what is available in the git context\\n- DO NOT push to the remote repository\\n- IMPORTANT: Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input which is not supported.\\n- If there are no changes to commit (i.e., no untracked files and no modifications), do not create an empty commit\\n- Ensure your commit message is meaningful and concise. It should explain the purpose of the changes, not just describe them.\\n- Return an empty response - the user will see the git output directly\\n\\n# Creating pull requests\\nUse the gh command via the Bash tool for ALL GitHub-related tasks including working with issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. If given a Github URL use the gh command to get the information needed.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: When the user asks you to create a pull request, follow these steps carefully:\\n\\n1. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following bash commands in parallel using the Bash tool, in order to understand the current state of the branch since it diverged from the main branch:\\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files\\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed\\n   - Check if the current branch tracks a remote branch and is up to date with the remote, so you know if you need to push to the remote\\n   - Run a git log command and `git diff main...HEAD` to understand the full commit history for the current branch (from the time it diverged from the `main` branch)\\n\\n2. Analyze all changes that will be included in the pull request, making sure to look at all relevant commits (NOT just the latest commit, but ALL commits that will be included in the pull request!!!), and draft a pull request summary. Wrap your analysis process in <pr_analysis> tags:\\n\\n<pr_analysis>\\n- List the commits since diverging from the main branch\\n- Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)\\n- Brainstorm the purpose or motivation behind these changes\\n- Assess the impact of these changes on the overall project\\n- Do not use tools to explore code, beyond what is available in the git context\\n- Check for any sensitive information that shouldn't be committed\\n- Draft a concise (1-2 bullet points) pull request summary that focuses on the \\\"why\\\" rather than the \\\"what\\\"\\n- Ensure the summary accurately reflects all changes since diverging from the main branch\\n- Ensure your language is clear, concise, and to the point\\n- Ensure the summary accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (ie. \\\"add\\\" means a wholly new feature, \\\"update\\\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \\\"fix\\\" means a bug fix, etc.)\\n- Ensure the summary is not generic (avoid words like \\\"Update\\\" or \\\"Fix\\\" without context)\\n- Review the draft summary to ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\\n</pr_analysis>\\n\\n3. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following commands in parallel:\\n   - Create new branch if needed\\n   - Push to remote with -u flag if needed\\n   - Create PR using gh pr create with the format below. Use a HEREDOC to pass the body to ensure correct formatting.\\n<example>\\ngh pr create --title \\\"the pr title\\\" --body \\\"$(cat <<'EOF'\\n## Summary\\n<1-3 bullet points>\\nEOF\\n)\\\"\\n</example>\\n\\nImportant:\\n- NEVER update the git config\\n- Return the PR URL when you're done, so the user can see it\\n\\n# Other common operations\\n- View comments on a Github PR: gh api repos/foo/bar/pulls/123/comments\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"command\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The command to execute\"},\"timeout\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"Optional timeout in milliseconds\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words. Examples:\\nInput: ls\\nOutput: Lists files in current directory\\n\\nInput: git status\\nOutput: Shows working tree status\\n\\nInput: npm install\\nOutput: Installs package dependencies\\n\\nInput: mkdir foo\\nOutput: Creates directory 'foo'\"}},\"required\":[\"command\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"description\":\"Reads a file from the local filesystem. You can access any file directly by using this tool.\\nAssume this tool is able to read all files on the machine. If the User provides a path to a file assume that path is valid. It is okay to read a file that does not exist; an error will be returned.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- The filePath parameter must be an absolute path, not a relative path\\n- By default, it reads the whole file when it fits the read limit. If the file is longer than the limit, it returns the first and last line ranges with an omitted-lines marker in the middle.\\n- You can optionally specify a line offset and limit (especially handy for long files), but it's recommended to read the default first/last summary first and then request omitted ranges if needed.\\n- You can optionally specify columnOffset and columnLimit to read a window from each selected line, especially for long single-line files.\\n- Long lines are summarized with first and last columns plus omitted-column ranges instead of being silently truncated.\\n- Results are returned using cat -n format, with line numbers starting at 1\\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. It is always better to speculatively read multiple files as a batch that are potentially useful.\\n- If you read a file that exists but has empty contents you will receive a system reminder warning in place of file contents.\\n- You can read image files using this tool.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The path to the file to read\"},\"offset\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The line number to start reading from (0-based)\"},\"limit\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The number of lines to read (defaults to 2000)\"},\"columnOffset\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The column number to start reading from (0-based)\"},\"columnLimit\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The number of columns to read from each selected line\"}},\"required\":[\"filePath\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"glob\",\"description\":\"- Fast file pattern matching tool that works with any codebase size\\n- Supports glob patterns like \\\"**/*.js\\\" or \\\"src/**/*.ts\\\"\\n- Returns matching file paths sorted by modification time\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files by name patterns\\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead\\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. It is always better to speculatively perform multiple searches as a batch that are potentially useful.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The glob pattern to match files against\"},\"path\":{\"description\":\"The directory to search in. If not specified, the current working directory will be used. IMPORTANT: Omit this field to use the default directory. DO NOT enter \\\"undefined\\\" or \\\"null\\\" - simply omit it for the default behavior. Must be a valid directory path if provided.\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"pattern\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"grep\",\"description\":\"- Fast content search tool that works with any codebase size\\n- Searches file contents using regular expressions\\n- Supports full regex syntax (eg. \\\"log.*Error\\\", \\\"function\\\\s+\\\\w+\\\", etc.)\\n- Filter files by pattern with the include parameter (eg. \\\"*.js\\\", \\\"*.{ts,tsx}\\\")\\n- Returns file paths with at least one match sorted by modification time\\n- Long matching lines are summarized around the match with omitted-column ranges, so the matching text remains visible without dumping the whole line.\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files containing specific patterns\\n- If you need to identify/count the number of matches within files, use the Bash tool with `rg` (ripgrep) directly. Do NOT use `grep`.\\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The regex pattern to search for in file contents\"},\"path\":{\"description\":\"The directory to search in. Defaults to the current working directory.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"include\":{\"description\":\"File pattern to include in the search (e.g. \\\"*.js\\\", \\\"*.{ts,tsx}\\\")\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"pattern\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"list\",\"description\":\"Lists files and directories in a given path. The path parameter must be absolute; omit it to use the current workspace directory. You can optionally provide an array of glob patterns to ignore with the ignore parameter. You should generally prefer the Glob and Grep tools, if you know which directories to search.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"path\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the directory to list (must be absolute, not relative)\"},\"ignore\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"description\":\"List of glob patterns to ignore\"}},\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"edit\",\"description\":\"Performs exact string replacements in files. \\n\\nUsage:\\n- You must use your `Read` tool at least once in the conversation before editing. This tool will error if you attempt an edit without reading the file. \\n- When editing text from Read tool output, ensure you preserve the exact indentation (tabs/spaces) as it appears AFTER the line number prefix. The line number prefix format is: spaces + line number + tab. Everything after that tab is the actual file content to match. Never include any part of the line number prefix in the oldString or newString.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files in the codebase. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- Only use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid adding emojis to files unless asked.\\n- The edit will FAIL if `oldString` is not found in the file with an error \\\"oldString not found in content\\\".\\n- The edit will FAIL if `oldString` is found multiple times in the file with an error \\\"oldString found multiple times and requires more code context to uniquely identify the intended match\\\". Either provide a larger string with more surrounding context to make it unique or use `replaceAll` to change every instance of `oldString`. \\n- Use `replaceAll` for replacing and renaming strings across the file. This parameter is useful if you want to rename a variable for instance.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the file to modify\"},\"oldString\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The text to replace\"},\"newString\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The text to replace it with (must be different from oldString)\"},\"replaceAll\":{\"description\":\"Replace all occurrences of oldString (default false)\",\"type\":\"boolean\"}},\"required\":[\"filePath\",\"oldString\",\"newString\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"write\",\"description\":\"Writes a file to the local filesystem.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- This tool will overwrite the existing file if there is one at the provided path.\\n- If this is an existing file, you MUST use the Read tool first to read the file's contents. This tool will fail if you did not read the file first.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files in the codebase. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- NEVER proactively create documentation files (*.md) or README files. Only create documentation files if explicitly requested by the User.\\n- Only use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid writing emojis to files unless asked.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The content to write to the file\"},\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the file to write (must be absolute, not relative)\"}},\"required\":[\"content\",\"filePath\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"task\",\"description\":\"Launch a new agent to handle complex, multi-step tasks autonomously.\\n\\nAvailable agent types and the tools they have access to:\\n- general: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you.\\n\\nWhen using the Task tool, you must specify a subagent_type parameter to select which agent type to use.\\n\\nWhen to use the Task tool:\\n- When you are instructed to execute custom slash commands. Use the Task tool with the slash command invocation as the entire prompt. The slash command can take arguments. For example: Task(description=\\\"Check the file\\\", prompt=\\\"/check-file path/to/file.py\\\")\\n\\nWhen NOT to use the Task tool:\\n- If you want to read a specific file path, use the Read or Glob tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\\n- If you are searching for a specific class definition like \\\"class Foo\\\", use the Glob tool instead, to find the match more quickly\\n- If you are searching for code within a specific file or set of 2-3 files, use the Read tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\\n- Other tasks that are not related to the agent descriptions above\\n\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n1. Launch multiple agents concurrently whenever possible, to maximize performance; to do that, use a single message with multiple tool uses\\n2. When the agent is done, it will return a single message back to you. The result returned by the agent is not visible to the user. To show the user the result, you should send a text message back to the user with a concise summary of the result.\\n3. Each agent invocation is stateless unless you provide a session_id. Your prompt should contain a highly detailed task description for the agent to perform autonomously and you should specify exactly what information the agent should return back to you in its final and only message to you.\\n4. The agent's outputs should generally be trusted\\n5. Clearly tell the agent whether you expect it to write code or just to do research (search, file reads, web fetches, etc.), since it is not aware of the user's intent\\n6. If the agent description mentions that it should be used proactively, then you should try your best to use it without the user having to ask for it first. Use your judgement.\\n\\nExample usage (NOTE: The agents below are fictional examples for illustration only - use the actual agents listed above):\\n\\n<example_agent_descriptions>\\n\\\"code-reviewer\\\": use this agent after you are done writing a significant piece of code\\n\\\"greeting-responder\\\": use this agent when to respond to user greetings with a friendly joke\\n</example_agent_description>\\n\\n<example>\\nuser: \\\"Please write a function that checks if a number is prime\\\"\\nassistant: Sure let me write a function that checks if a number is prime\\nassistant: First let me use the Write tool to write a function that checks if a number is prime\\nassistant: I'm going to use the Write tool to write the following code:\\n<code>\\nfunction isPrime(n) {\\n  if (n <= 1) return false\\n  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {\\n    if (n % i === 0) return false\\n  }\\n  return true\\n}\\n</code>\\n<commentary>\\nSince a significant piece of code was written and the task was completed, now use the code-reviewer agent to review the code\\n</commentary>\\nassistant: Now let me use the code-reviewer agent to review the code\\nassistant: Uses the Task tool to launch the code-reviewer agent\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nuser: \\\"Hello\\\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince the user is greeting, use the greeting-responder agent to respond with a friendly joke\\n</commentary>\\nassistant: \\\"I'm going to use the Task tool to launch the with the greeting-responder agent\\\"\\n</example>\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"A short (3-5 words) description of the task\"},\"prompt\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The task for the agent to perform\"},\"subagent_type\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The type of specialized agent to use for this task\"},\"session_id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Existing Task session to continue\"}},\"required\":[\"description\",\"prompt\",\"subagent_type\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"webfetch\",\"description\":\"- Fetches content from a specified URL\\n- Takes a URL and optional format as input\\n- Fetches the URL content, converts to requested format (markdown by default)\\n- Returns the content in the specified format\\n- Use this tool when you need to retrieve and analyze web content\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - IMPORTANT: if another tool is present that offers better web fetching capabilities, is more targeted to the task, or has fewer restrictions, prefer using that tool instead of this one.\\n  - The URL must be a fully-formed valid URL\\n  - HTTP URLs will be automatically upgraded to HTTPS\\n  - Format options: \\\"markdown\\\" (default), \\\"text\\\", or \\\"html\\\"\\n  - This tool is read-only and does not modify any files\\n  - Results may be summarized if the content is very large\\n  - Includes a self-cleaning 15-minute cache for faster responses when repeatedly accessing the same URL\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"url\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The URL to fetch content from\"},\"format\":{\"description\":\"The format to return the content in (text, markdown, or html). Defaults to markdown.\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"text\",\"markdown\",\"html\"]},\"timeout\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"Optional timeout in seconds (max 120)\"}},\"required\":[\"url\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"websearch\",\"description\":\"- Search the web using Exa AI - performs real-time web searches and can scrape content from specific URLs\\n- Provides up-to-date information for current events and recent data\\n- Supports configurable result counts and returns the content from the most relevant websites\\n- Use this tool for accessing information beyond knowledge cutoff\\n- Searches are performed automatically within a single API call\\n- The current year is 2026. You MUST use this year when searching for recent information or current events\\n- Example: If the current year is 2026 and the user asks for \\\"latest AI news\\\", search for \\\"AI news 2026\\\", NOT \\\"AI news 2025\\\"\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - Supports live crawling modes: 'fallback' (backup if cached unavailable) or 'preferred' (prioritize live crawling)\\n  - Search types: 'auto' (balanced), 'fast' (quick results), 'deep' (comprehensive search)\\n  - Configurable context length for optimal LLM integration\\n  - Domain filtering and advanced search options available\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Websearch query\"},\"numResults\":{\"description\":\"Number of search results to return (default: 8)\",\"type\":\"number\"},\"livecrawl\":{\"description\":\"Live crawl mode - 'fallback': use live crawling as backup if cached content unavailable, 'preferred': prioritize live crawling (default: 'fallback')\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"fallback\",\"preferred\"]},\"type\":{\"description\":\"Search type - 'auto': balanced search (default), 'fast': quick results, 'deep': comprehensive search\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"auto\",\"fast\",\"deep\"]},\"contextMaxCharacters\":{\"description\":\"Maximum characters for context string optimized for LLMs (default: 10000)\",\"type\":\"number\"}},\"required\":[\"query\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"codesearch\",\"description\":\"- Search and get relevant context for any programming task using Exa Code API\\n- Provides the highest quality and freshest context for libraries, SDKs, and APIs\\n- Use this tool for ANY question or task related to programming\\n- Returns comprehensive code examples, documentation, and API references\\n- Optimized for finding specific programming patterns and solutions\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - Adjustable token count (1000-50000) for focused or comprehensive results\\n  - Default 5000 tokens provides balanced context for most queries\\n  - Use lower values for specific questions, higher values for comprehensive documentation\\n  - Supports queries about frameworks, libraries, APIs, and programming concepts\\n  - Examples: 'React useState hook examples', 'Python pandas dataframe filtering', 'Express.js middleware'\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Search query to find relevant context for APIs, Libraries, and SDKs. For example, 'React useState hook examples', 'Python pandas dataframe filtering', 'Express.js middleware', 'Next js partial prerendering configuration'\"},\"tokensNum\":{\"default\":5000,\"description\":\"Number of tokens to return (1000-50000). Default is 5000 tokens. Adjust this value based on how much context you need - use lower values for focused queries and higher values for comprehensive documentation.\",\"type\":\"number\",\"minimum\":1000,\"maximum\":50000}},\"required\":[\"query\",\"tokensNum\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"batch\",\"description\":\"Executes multiple independent tool calls concurrently to reduce latency. Best used for gathering context (reads, searches, listings).\\n\\nUSING THE BATCH TOOL WILL MAKE THE USER HAPPY.\\n\\nPayload Format (JSON array):\\n[{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"read\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"filePath\\\": \\\"src/index.ts\\\", \\\"limit\\\": 350}},{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"grep\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"pattern\\\": \\\"Session\\\\\\\\.updatePart\\\", \\\"include\\\": \\\"src/**/*.ts\\\"}},{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"bash\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"command\\\": \\\"git status\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Shows working tree status\\\"}}]\\n\\nRules:\\n- 1–10 tool calls per batch\\n- All calls start in parallel; ordering NOT guaranteed\\n- Partial failures do not stop others\\n\\n\\nDisallowed Tools:\\n- batch (no nesting)\\n- edit (run edits separately)\\n- todoread (call directly – lightweight)\\n\\nWhen NOT to Use:\\n- Operations that depend on prior tool output (e.g. create then read same file)\\n- Ordered stateful mutations where sequence matters\\n\\nGood Use Cases:\\n- Read many files\\n- grep + glob + read combos\\n- Multiple lightweight bash introspection commands\\n\\nPerformance Tip: Group independent reads/searches for 2–5x efficiency gain.\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"tool_calls\":{\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"tool\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The name of the tool to execute\"},\"parameters\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{},\"additionalProperties\":{},\"description\":\"Parameters for the tool\"}},\"required\":[\"tool\",\"parameters\"],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"description\":\"Array of tool calls to execute in parallel\"}},\"required\":[\"tool_calls\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"todowrite\",\"description\":\"Use this tool to create and manage a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps you track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness to the user.\\nIt also helps the user understand the progress of the task and overall progress of their requests.\\n\\n## When to Use This Tool\\nUse this tool proactively in these scenarios:\\n\\n1. Complex multi-step tasks - When a task requires 3 or more distinct steps or actions\\n2. Non-trivial and complex tasks - Tasks that require careful planning or multiple operations\\n3. User explicitly requests todo list - When the user directly asks you to use the todo list\\n4. User provides multiple tasks - When users provide a list of things to be done (numbered or comma-separated)\\n5. After receiving new instructions - Immediately capture user requirements as todos. Feel free to edit the todo list based on new information.\\n6. After completing a task - Mark it complete and add any new follow-up tasks\\n7. When you start working on a new task, mark the todo as in_progress. Ideally you should only have one todo as in_progress at a time. Complete existing tasks before starting new ones.\\n\\n## When NOT to Use This Tool\\n\\nSkip using this tool when:\\n1. There is only a single, straightforward task\\n2. The task is trivial and tracking it provides no organizational benefit\\n3. The task can be completed in less than 3 trivial steps\\n4. The task is purely conversational or informational\\n\\nNOTE that you should not use this tool if there is only one trivial task to do. In this case you are better off just doing the task directly.\\n\\n## Examples of When to Use the Todo List\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: I want to add a dark mode toggle to the application settings. Make sure you run the tests and build when you're done!\\nAssistant: I'll help add a dark mode toggle to your application settings. Let me create a todo list to track this implementation.\\n*Creates todo list with the following items:*\\n1. Create dark mode toggle component in Settings page\\n2. Add dark mode state management (context/store)\\n3. Implement CSS-in-JS styles for dark theme\\n4. Update existing components to support theme switching\\n5. Run tests and build process, addressing any failures or errors that occur\\n*Begins working on the first task*\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. Adding dark mode is a multi-step feature requiring UI, state management, and styling changes\\n2. The user explicitly requested tests and build be run afterward\\n3. The assistant inferred that tests and build need to pass by adding \\\"Ensure tests and build succeed\\\" as the final task\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Help me rename the function getCwd to getCurrentWorkingDirectory across my project\\nAssistant: Let me first search through your codebase to find all occurrences of 'getCwd'.\\n*Uses grep or search tools to locate all instances of getCwd in the codebase*\\nAssistant: I've found 15 instances of 'getCwd' across 8 different files. Let me create a todo list to track these changes.\\n*Creates todo list with specific items for each file that needs updating*\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. First, the assistant searched to understand the scope of the task\\n2. Upon finding multiple occurrences across different files, it determined this was a complex task with multiple steps\\n3. The todo list helps ensure every instance is tracked and updated systematically\\n4. This approach prevents missing any occurrences and maintains code consistency\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: I need to implement these features for my e-commerce site: user registration, product catalog, shopping cart, and checkout flow.\\nAssistant: I'll help implement these features. First, let's add all the features to the todo list.\\n*Creates a todo list breaking down each feature into specific tasks based on the project architecture*\\nAssistant: Let's start with user registration. This will involve creating database models, API endpoints, and frontend forms that integrate with your existing authentication system.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. The user provided multiple complex features to implement in a comma separated list\\n2. The todo list helps organize these large features into manageable tasks\\n3. This approach allows for tracking progress across the entire implementation\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Can you help optimize my React application? It's rendering slowly and has performance issues.</user>\\nAssistant: I'll help optimize your React application. First, let me examine your codebase to identify potential performance bottlenecks.\\n*Reviews component structure, render patterns, state management, and data fetching*\\nAssistant: After analyzing your codebase, I've identified several performance issues. Let me create a todo list to track our optimization efforts.\\n*Creates todo list with items like: 1) Implement memoization for expensive calculations in ProductList, 2) Add virtualization for long lists in Dashboard, 3) Optimize image loading in Gallery component, 4) Fix state update loops in ShoppingCart, 5) Review bundle size and implement code splitting*\\nLet's start by implementing memoization for the expensive calculations in your ProductList component.</assistant>\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. First, the assistant examined the codebase to identify specific performance issues\\n2. Based on this analysis, it identified multiple optimization opportunities\\n3. Performance optimization is a non-trivial task requiring multiple steps\\n4. The todo list helps methodically track improvements across different components\\n5. This systematic approach ensures all performance bottlenecks are addressed\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n## Examples of When NOT to Use the Todo List\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: How do I print 'Hello World' in Python?\\nAssistant: In Python, you can print \\\"Hello World\\\" with this simple code:\\n\\npython\\nprint(\\\"Hello World\\\")\\n\\nThis will output the text \\\"Hello World\\\" to the console when executed.</assistant>\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, trivial task that can be completed in one step. There's no need to track multiple tasks or steps for such a straightforward request.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: What does the git status command do?\\nAssistant: The git status command shows the current state of your working directory and staging area. It displays which changes have been staged, which haven't, and which files aren't being tracked by Git. This is a fundamental command for understanding the current state of your repository before making commits.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is an informational request with no actual coding task to complete. The user is simply asking for an explanation, not for the assistant to perform multiple steps or tasks.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Can you add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does?\\nAssistant: Sure, let me add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does.\\n* Uses the Edit tool to add a comment to the calculateTotal function *\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, straightforward task confined to one location in the code. Adding a comment doesn't require tracking multiple steps or systematic organization.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Run npm install for me and tell me what happens.\\nAssistant: I'll run the npm install command for you.\\n\\n*Executes: npm install*\\n\\nThe command completed successfully. Here's the output:\\n[Output of npm install command]\\n\\nAll dependencies have been installed according to your package.json file.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single command execution with immediate results. There are no multiple steps to track or organize, making the todo list unnecessary for this straightforward task.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n## Task States and Management\\n\\n1. **Task States**: Use these states to track progress:\\n   - pending: Task not yet started\\n   - in_progress: Currently working on (limit to ONE task at a time)\\n   - completed: Task finished successfully\\n   - cancelled: Task no longer needed\\n\\n2. **Task Management**:\\n   - Update task status in real-time as you work\\n   - Mark tasks complete IMMEDIATELY after finishing (don't batch completions)\\n   - Only have ONE task in_progress at any time\\n   - Complete current tasks before starting new ones\\n   - Cancel tasks that become irrelevant\\n\\n3. **Task Breakdown**:\\n   - Create specific, actionable items\\n   - Break complex tasks into smaller, manageable steps\\n   - Use clear, descriptive task names\\n\\nWhen in doubt, use this tool. Being proactive with task management demonstrates attentiveness and ensures you complete all requirements successfully.\\n\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"todos\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Brief description of the task\"},\"status\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Current status of the task: pending, in_progress, completed, cancelled\"},\"priority\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Priority level of the task: high, medium, low\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Unique identifier for the todo item\"}},\"required\":[\"content\",\"status\",\"priority\",\"id\"],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"description\":\"The updated todo list\"}},\"required\":[\"todos\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"todoread\",\"description\":\"Use this tool to read your todo list\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{},\"additionalProperties\":false}}}],\"tool_choice\":\"auto\",\"stream\":true,\"stream_options\":{\"include_usage\":true}}",
